WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Pressure is part of the drama, but pressure without boundaries becomes noise.

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:20.000
TABLE PULSE exists to keep the room tense, alive, and readable without turning the session into a dashboard performance.

3
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The GM sees the signal first. The system offers pressure, reaction, and aftermath as packets, not commandments.

4
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:40.000
The table can be nudged when a scene needs oxygen and left alone when silence is the better choice.

5
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Players who are not physically in the room can still matter when they join an opposing faction.

6
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:00.000
If they opt in, they can receive a bounded notification, send a reaction, and push back from outside the table without hijacking the moment inside it.

7
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.000
After the run, they can receive a focused summary of what happened, who won, and what fallout now belongs to their side.

8
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Consent, quiet hours, opt-outs, and table policy are not decoration around the system. They are the system.

9
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:30.000
A good pulse is felt in the scene, in the aftermath, and in the rising tension of the campaign, while the software itself almost disappears.
